"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms=globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms||[]).push([[2565],{8687(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"Backtracking","permalink":"/concepts/tags/backtracking","allTagsPath":"/concepts/tags","count":4,"items":[{"id":"state-space-search-and-optimization/backtracking","title":"Backtracking","description":"Some problems resist every technique seen so far. There is no greedy criterion that guarantees the optimum, the problem does not split into independent halves, and there is no small table of subproblems to fill. Very often the only way to be sure of the answer is to examine the candidate solutions one by one. Backtracking is the paradigm that organizes that examination so that it is systematic (no candidate is forgotten) and as cheap as possible (whole families of candidates are discarded at once).","permalink":"/concepts/state-space-search-and-optimization/backtracking"},{"id":"state-space-search-and-optimization/backtracking-examples","title":"Backtracking: Classic Problems","description":"This lesson applies the general schemes of backtracking to a series of classic problems. They are ordered from simple to more demanding, and each one illustrates a different aspect of the paradigm:","permalink":"/concepts/state-space-search-and-optimization/backtracking-examples"},{"id":"state-space-search-and-optimization/exercises","title":"Exercises: Backtracking and Branch and Bound","description":"These exercises review the four lessons of the chapter. They are different from the seminar activities (paths, Hamiltonian cycles and the traveling salesman) and from the lab problems, which are left for those sessions. Try each one before reading its solution: the solutions are grouped at the end of the page.","permalink":"/concepts/state-space-search-and-optimization/exercises"},{"id":"state-space-search-and-optimization/proofs","title":"Proofs: Backtracking and Branch and Bound","description":"Backtracking and branch and bound are exact methods: they promise to find every solution, or the optimal one. That promise depends on the pruning being safe, that is, never discarding a branch that contains what we are looking for. This page proves when that is the case, and proves the specific facts used in the examples.","permalink":"/concepts/state-space-search-and-optimization/proofs"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);